<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ridder-title">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ridder</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Knight, Chevalier, Ridderschap</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ridder, meaning knight, is a noble title in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, a literal translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> eques, originally 'horseman' or 'rider'. Before 1814 it was the highest rank of the nobility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, below only the head of state, and the knights of each province formed a ridderschap, which after the abjuration of Philip II in 1581 held much executive and legislative power. The revolution of 1798 ended that power. In modern usage ridder is the second lowest rank, below baron and above the untitled jonkheer; it has no female form in the Netherlands or Belgium, and the title descends either to all male-line descendants or to the eldest alone.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ridder-title.jpg" alt="Ridder" width="330" height="541" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IDD5000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sint-Martinuskerk_(Aalst)_-_Grafmonument_van_Ridder_Du_Bosch_en_Isabeau_Lotin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
